EXT. SALT-WORKS BOILING SHED - ABANDONED TERRITORY - DAY

Wind-scoured plank walls. Iron evaporation pans crusted white. A lone RIDER (40s, flour-sack coat, hat pulled low) leads a lame horse through the doorway and ties it to a post.

He paces the shed, reading the walls. A faded wanted poster flaps from a nail. He tears it free and studies it.

                    RIDER
          (reading, low)
          Jedediah Strake. Five hundred dollars. Dead or alive.

He folds the poster into his coat. The scrape of a boot outside. He stops. Hand to his holster.

                    RIDER (CONT'D)
          (calling out)
          Whoever's there, I've got a crooked horse and a straight gun.

Silence. Wind. Then a voice, thin and tinny, from the saddlebag. He stares at it.

                    RADIO (V.O.)
          (through static, his own recorded voice)
          Jed. If you're hearing this, you've gone back to the salt.

He pulls a battered wax cylinder player from the bag, cranks it, and holds it up.

                    JEDEDIAH (V.O.)
          (recorded)
          Don't stay past dark. The pans remember.

Jedediah sets the player down. The horse whinnies. Outside, the sun drops behind the ridge, and the white pans turn gray, then blue, then slowly begin to glow.

                    JEDEDIAH
          (muttering)
          Well, I never did listen to myself.

He draws his pistol and steps out to meet the light.
